// 인용은 notes/web/<id>.txt 에 글자 그대로 있다(공백 차이 제외). 그림 값(FIG A/B/C)은 notes/figures.md 에 있다.
{"id": "e1", "source": "w1", "locator": "§intro", "quote": "At a high level, I found that effort was a great way of modulating how much verification and edgecase testing Claude did and how much of its own judgement it used.", "why": "the author's one-line answer to what effort changes"}
{"id": "e2", "source": "w1", "locator": "§intro", "quote": "Extra effort gave better results in domains where verification and edgecase testing was more useful like hardware, code review, and security.", "why": "where effort pays, per the author"}
{"id": "e3", "source": "w1", "locator": "§intro", "quote": "For normal software engineering, I’m running a loop of getting the model to interview me then implementing on low effort, reviewing what it built, and then running verification on high effort.", "why": "author's own loop"}
{"id": "e4", "source": "w1", "locator": "§intro", "quote": "One of the best parts of our newest Claude models is how they respond to effort without breaking the prompt cache in Claude Code", "why": "effort change does not break cache"}
{"id": "e5", "source": "w1", "locator": "§What is effort?", "quote": "At a high level, effort gives the model an approximation of how much compute you want it to spend on the task. It’s somewhat related to your modeling of the difficulty of the task.", "why": "definition"}
{"id": "e6", "source": "w1", "locator": "§What is effort?", "quote": "Claude will always try and do your task reasonably, but higher effort will involve Claude taking more independent action for judgement and verification.", "why": "mechanism: independent action"}
{"id": "e7", "source": "w1", "locator": "§What is effort?", "quote": "if someone asked you to do something in 12 hours straight, you might assume they just want you to do it and try very hard. If someone asked you to do the same task in 1 hour, you’d try to get them the best version that meets their task and then expect to iterate from there.", "why": "the analogy"}
{"id": "e8", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Effort curves", "quote": "Fable 5.1 and Opus 5.5’s effort curves are our best yet: at each level, there is an uptick in benchmark scores and tokens consumed.", "why": "curve claim"}
{"id": "e9", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Effort curves FIG A caption", "quote": "Terminal-Bench 3.0, the same 70 tasks for every model (the 4 GPU tasks are left out). Opus 5.5 ran about three weeks later, with responses capped at 128k tokens and no GitHub or PyPI access; Opus 5’s max is its effort-120 run.", "why": "FIG A conditions"}
{"id": "e10", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Building with effort", "quote": "If I ask Claude to “build a personal fitness and workout tracker app,” effort changes dramatically how fleshed out the app is, but also results in Claude making more choices along the way.", "why": "underspecified build"}
{"id": "e11", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Building with effort", "quote": "At low effort, the fitness app is just a log and a simple graph. At higher effort levels the app is more complex with additional detail. At max effort there’s a heat chart.", "why": "underspecified build detail"}
{"id": "e12", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Building with effort", "quote": "low(1.5 min)medium(4 min)high(11 min)max(67 min)", "why": "underspecified build times"}
{"id": "e13", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Building with effort", "quote": "At low effort (which took 1 minute), I got an interactive sketch that conveyed the idea but didn’t look very much like Claude Code.", "why": "/config low"}
{"id": "e14", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Building with effort", "quote": "At max effort (which took 28 minutes), I got a mockup that looked very much like Claude Code, along with a bunch of walkthroughs for different flows.", "why": "/config max"}
{"id": "e15", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Building with effort", "quote": "For this particular task, I think I prefer using low effort to understand Claude’s vision.", "why": "/config preference"}
{"id": "e16", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Building with effort", "quote": "I found that given this spec, the models behaved much more similarly. I got designs that looked fairly similar and had similar implementations but with different details", "why": "spec build converges"}
{"id": "e17", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Building with effort", "quote": "low(16 min)medium(22 min)high(33 min)max(79 min)", "why": "spec build times"}
{"id": "e18", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Takeaways", "quote": "Low effort allows Claude to respond quickly with a starting point, higher effort levels will get more work done but Claude will also make more assumptions on my behalf.", "why": "assumptions on behalf"}
{"id": "e19", "source": "w1", "locator": "§Takeaways", "quote": "For regular software engineering, particularly new feature work, the effort level depends a lot on how in the loop I want to be.", "why": "in the loop"}
{"id": "e20", "source": "w1", "locator": "§How effort levels impact output", "quote": "My main takeaway from reading the Terminal-Bench 3.0 results was that higher effort is best for tasks with lots of hidden edge cases.", "why": "main takeaway"}
{"id": "e21", "source": "w1", "locator": "§How effort levels impact output", "quote": "A clean example is html-js-filter, a Terminal-Bench 3.0 task that asks for an HTML sanitizer that strips every way of smuggling JavaScript into a page. Fable 5.1 went from 1/5 at low to 5/5 at xhigh.", "why": "html-js-filter"}
{"id": "e22", "source": "w1", "locator": "§How effort levels impact output", "quote": "A typical attempt at low effort takes about 2 minutes. Each of these attempts wrote a filter in roughly one pass, then tested it against a single hand-written page.", "why": "html-js-filter low"}
{"id": "e23", "source": "w1", "locator": "§How effort levels impact output", "quote": "A high-effort run finishes in about 33 minutes. In the run I traced, it adversarially reviewed its first draft, then read the installed parser’s source to check for bugs", "why": "html-js-filter high"}
{"id": "e24", "source": "w1", "locator": "§How effort levels impact output", "quote": "ran a standard XSS test suite, and finally, wrote a random-document fuzzer.", "why": "html-js-filter high end"}
{"id": "e25", "source": "w1", "locator": "§How effort levels impact output", "quote": "Overall, increasing effort tends to reduce failures due to missing edgecases (purple blocks), but does not fix when the model has the wrong approach (blue blocks).", "why": "FIG B claim"}
{"id": "e26", "source": "w1", "locator": "§FIG B caption", "quote": "Each square is one attempt. Failure kinds come from a model judge and are approximate.", "why": "FIG B judge"}
{"id": "e27", "source": "w1", "locator": "§FIG C caption", "quote": "Pass rate. Low effort pools each model's two lowest settings, top effort its three highest, since categories are small.", "why": "FIG C pooling"}
{"id": "e28", "source": "w1", "locator": "§FIG C", "quote": "Fable 5.1, pass rate at low effort → top effort: Security 64% → 87%, Hardware 34% → 75%, ML 54% → 73%, Science 41% → 61%, Software 43% → 56%, Media 18% → 30%, Operations 12% → 22%.", "why": "FIG C Fable 5.1 summary"}
{"id": "e29", "source": "w1", "locator": "§FIG C", "quote": "Operations10 tasks12% → 22% Rulebook-style work stays low", "why": "FIG C operations annotation"}
{"id": "e30", "source": "w1", "locator": "§FIG B", "quote": "Fable 5.1 at low370 attempts, median 73k tokens eachFable 5.1 at max370 attempts, median 222k tokens each", "why": "FIG B tokens"}
{"id": "e31", "source": "w1", "locator": "§FIG B", "quote": "passed140 → 214missed a case59 → 24works on my machine2 → 1overfit to the examples7 → 3", "why": "FIG B pass & missed"}
{"id": "e32", "source": "w1", "locator": "§FIG B", "quote": "other failures38 → 25made the wrong call133 → 107misread a requirement45 → 26", "why": "FIG B wrong call"}
{"id": "e33", "source": "w1", "locator": "§FIG B", "quote": "got the domain rule wrong32 → 24picked the wrong reading25 → 47", "why": "FIG B wrong reading"}
{"id": "e34", "source": "w1", "locator": "§mvcc-lsm-compaction", "quote": "Opus 5.5 went from 0/5 at low to 4/5 at xhigh.", "why": "mvcc numbers"}
{"id": "e35", "source": "w1", "locator": "§mvcc-lsm-compaction", "quote": "At low (about a minute per attempt), Claude would edit the code before building it or running the reproducer, and did not check that its new test would have caught the original bug.", "why": "mvcc low"}
{"id": "e36", "source": "w1", "locator": "§mvcc-lsm-compaction", "quote": "At xhigh (about 11 minutes), Claude reproduced the crash first, wrote a randomized test against a reference that never compacts, and checked that its tests failed on half-finished fixes.", "why": "mvcc xhigh"}
{"id": "e37", "source": "w1", "locator": "§cli-2ph-simplex", "quote": "a Terminal-Bench 3.0 task that asks for a CLI linear-program solver written in Python. Opus 5.5 went from 0/5 at low to 5/5 at high.", "why": "simplex numbers"}
{"id": "e38", "source": "w1", "locator": "§cli-2ph-simplex", "quote": "The low attempts wrote a solver in one pass, checked it with a few small problems and stopped around 10k tokens. In the final message, Claude warned it might be slow on big problems, but did not check.", "why": "simplex low"}
{"id": "e39", "source": "w1", "locator": "§cli-2ph-simplex", "quote": "During the high attempts, Claude tested its solver on random problems against a separate brute-force solver, then timed bigger ones, hit cases that ran far too long or crashed, and reworked its search.", "why": "simplex high"}
{"id": "e40", "source": "w1", "locator": "§gsea-proteomics", "quote": "to find which of eight treatments resemble a target tissue. Opus 5.5 went from 0/5 at low to 4/5 at high.", "why": "gsea numbers"}
{"id": "e41", "source": "w1", "locator": "§gsea-proteomics", "quote": "At high, Claude tried two ways of prepping the data, noticed that the list of significant treatments changed, and dug into why before choosing the correct one.", "why": "gsea high"}
{"id": "e42", "source": "w1", "locator": "§gsea-proteomics", "quote": "If a user were in the loop, Claude may have asked the user about the way to set up the problem, but without a user in the loop, high effort does better.", "why": "gsea user in loop"}
{"id": "e43", "source": "w1", "locator": "§When to use different effort levels", "quote": "Low: for when I want quick responses that are in the loop, e.g. brainstorming, sketching, easy changes", "why": "rule low"}
{"id": "e44", "source": "w1", "locator": "§When to use different effort levels", "quote": "Medium: for most of my regular software engineering work, e.g. new feature implementation.", "why": "rule medium"}
{"id": "e45", "source": "w1", "locator": "§When to use different effort levels", "quote": "High: for work where verification is important or there are edge cases, e.g. fixing a bug in a brownfield codebase.", "why": "rule high"}
{"id": "e46", "source": "w1", "locator": "§When to use different effort levels", "quote": "Max: When I want Claude to operate fully autonomously to solve difficult problems, e.g. end to end building and verification of an app, finding security vulnerabilities in critical software.", "why": "rule max"}
{"id": "e47", "source": "w1", "locator": "§footnote 1", "quote": "these come from our own internal runs, 5 attempts per task, with our production safety interventions off for Fable 5.1; in Claude products, Fable 5.1’s safeguards hand some security requests to Opus.", "why": "footnote conditions"}
{"id": "e48", "source": "w1", "locator": "§footnote 1", "quote": "The security tasks also ran without internet access, so the per-task counts here won’t line up with the public leaderboard or the launch post. The worked examples come from individual runs, some at intermediate effort settings.", "why": "footnote conditions 2"}
{"id": "e49", "source": "w1", "locator": "§byline", "quote": "What effort really is and when to use which level in Claude Code, from my own tests of three builds and a deep dive into Terminal-Bench 3.0 on Opus 5.5 and Fable 5.1.", "why": "subtitle"}
{"id": "e50", "source": "w1", "locator": "FIG A aria-label", "quote": "Opus 5.5 scores highest at every setting, from 36.6% at low to 65.7% at max, and Fable 5.1 scores higher than Opus 5 and Fable 5 at every setting.", "why": "FIG A endpoints"}
