{"id": "e78", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Adjust effort level", "quote": "[Effort levels](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/effort) control adaptive reasoning, which lets the model decide whether and how much to think on each step based on task complexity.", "why": "what effort controls in Claude Code"}
{"id": "e79", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Adjust effort level", "quote": "3. The model's default effort: `high` on every model that supports effort, except that Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5, and Haiku 5.5 default to `medium`, Opus 4.7 defaults to `xhigh`", "why": "defaults"}
{"id": "e80", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Adjust effort level", "quote": "Opus 5.5 starts at `medium` unless one of the sources above sets a level for it, and a top-level `effortLevel` in your user settings file doesn't count for Opus 5.5.", "why": "effortLevel not for Opus 5.5"}
{"id": "e81", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Adjust effort level", "quote": "* `s` in the `/effort` slider or the `/model` picker: apply the level to this session only. Requires Claude Code v2.1.257 or later", "why": "s = session only"}
{"id": "e82", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Adjust effort level", "quote": "* `Enter` in the `/effort` slider or the `/model` picker, or a level typed after `/effort`: save the level as your default and apply it in later sessions", "why": "Enter = save default"}
{"id": "e83", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Adjust effort level", "quote": "`max` is the deepest reasoning level. Unless you set it through the `CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL` environment variable, Claude Code applies `max` to the current session only.", "why": "max session only"}
{"id": "e84", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Choose an effort level", "quote": "The effort scale is calibrated per model, so the same level name does not represent the same underlying value across models.", "why": "per-model calibration"}
{"id": "e85", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Choose an effort level", "quote": "| `max` | Hard problems you want Claude to work through without you, such as finding security vulnerabilities. `max` may show diminishing returns and is prone to overthinking, so test before adopting it broadly |", "why": "max caveat in docs"}
{"id": "e86", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Choose an effort level", "quote": "In Anthropic's testing, Opus 5.5 at `medium` matches or exceeds Opus 5 at `high` on coding and knowledge-work evaluations. At a given level, Opus 5.5 tends to think more per turn than Opus 5.", "why": "Opus 5.5 vs 5"}
{"id": "e87", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Choose an effort level", "quote": "When you move from Opus 5 to Opus 5.5, start at `medium` rather than carrying over the level you used on Opus 5.", "why": "migration advice"}
{"id": "e88", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Use ultrathink", "quote": "Include `ultrathink` anywhere in your prompt to request deeper reasoning on that turn without changing your session effort setting. Claude Code recognizes the keyword and adds an in-context instruction. The effort level sent to the API is unchanged.", "why": "ultrathink"}
{"id": "e89", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Set the effort level", "quote": "You can run it while Claude is working, and once you confirm the [cache warning](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/prompt-caching.md#changing-effort-level), if Claude Code shows one, Claude Code applies the new level to the next request in the turn", "why": "mid-turn /effort and cache warning"}
{"id": "e90", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Set the effort level", "quote": "* **Skill and subagent frontmatter**: set `effort` in a [skill](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills.md#frontmatter-reference) or [subagent](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents.md#supported-frontmatter-fields) markdown file to override the effort level when that skill or subagent runs", "why": "frontmatter effort"}
{"id": "e91", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Set the effort level", "quote": "`max` isn't accepted as a level in either key", "why": "max not in settings"}
{"id": "e92", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Extended thinking", "quote": "You can't turn thinking off on Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5, Haiku 5.5, or the Fable models.", "why": "thinking cannot be off"}
{"id": "e93", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Adjust effort level", "quote": "If you want Claude to think more or less often than the current level produces, you can say so directly in your prompt or in `CLAUDE.md`; the model responds to that guidance within its effort setting.", "why": "prompt steering within effort"}
{"id": "e94", "source": "w3", "locator": "§How effort works", "quote": "Because effort applies to every output token, it works with or without thinking. Lower effort also means fewer and terser tool calls.", "why": "all output tokens"}
{"id": "e95", "source": "w3", "locator": "§Effort levels", "quote": "Effort is a behavioral signal, not a strict token budget. At lower effort levels, Claude still thinks on sufficiently difficult problems, but thinks less than it would at higher effort levels for the same problem.", "why": "behavioral signal"}
{"id": "e96", "source": "w3", "locator": "§Effort with tool use", "quote": "When using tools, the effort parameter affects both the explanations around tool calls and the tool calls themselves.", "why": "tool use"}
{"id": "e97", "source": "w3", "locator": "§Recommended effort levels for Claude Opus 5.5", "quote": "Claude Opus 5.5 also supports [changing effort mid-conversation](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/effort#change-effort-mid-conversation-beta) with a per-message `output_config`, which preserves the prompt cache.", "why": "per-message effort keeps cache"}
{"id": "e98", "source": "w3", "locator": "§Change effort mid-conversation", "quote": "On other models, set a new top-level value on the next request, which starts the cache over.", "why": "other models restart cache"}
{"id": "e99", "source": "w3", "locator": "§Change effort mid-conversation", "quote": "A top-level change restarts the cache and, on Claude Fable 5.1, also steers the model less reliably: its earlier replies were written at the previous level, and it tends to stay consistent with them.", "why": "why switch per message rather than top-level"}
{"id": "e100", "source": "w3", "locator": "§Change effort mid-conversation", "quote": "On Claude Fable 5.1, Claude Mythos 5.1, Claude Opus 5.5, Claude Opus 5, Claude Sonnet 5.5, and Claude Haiku 5.5, use a per-message effort change, which keeps the prompt cache.", "why": "which models keep cache on effort change"}
{"id": "e101", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Choose an effort level", "quote": "In tests on Opus 5.5 and Fable 5.1, Claude at a higher level tested more edge cases and verified more of its work before answering. It also made more choices on its own.", "why": "official docs restate the blog's finding"}
{"id": "e102", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Choose an effort level", "quote": "| `low` | Quick exchanges where you review each result, such as brainstorming, a first sketch, or a small change like a rename |", "why": "docs low row"}
{"id": "e103", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Choose an effort level", "quote": "| `high` | Work where verification matters or edge cases are likely, such as fixing a bug in an existing codebase. The default on every model except Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5, Haiku 5.5, and Opus 4.7 |", "why": "docs high row"}
{"id": "e104", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Choose an effort level", "quote": "| `xhigh` | Deeper reasoning at higher token spend. The default on Opus 4.7 |", "why": "docs xhigh row"}
{"id": "e105", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Set the effort level", "quote": "Frontmatter effort applies when that skill or subagent is active, overriding the session level but not the environment variable.", "why": "frontmatter precedence"}
{"id": "e106", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Extended thinking", "quote": "On these models, the model decides per step how much to think based on the effort level.", "why": "per-step thinking decided by effort"}
{"id": "e107", "source": "w3", "locator": "§Effort levels", "quote": "Long-running agentic and coding tasks (over 30 minutes) with token budgets in the millions", "why": "API doc's use case for xhigh"}
{"id": "e108", "source": "w4", "locator": "§Calibrate effort ¶1", "quote": "Start at `medium`, the default on Claude Opus 5.5 (Claude Opus 5 defaults to `high`), set it explicitly, and test several levels against your own evals rather than carrying over the setting you used on Claude Opus 5.", "why": "the calibration procedure's first step: start at medium, sweep levels on own evals"}
{"id": "e109", "source": "w4", "locator": "§Calibrate effort ¶1", "quote": "Effort level names don't correspond to the same amount of thinking across models: in Anthropic's testing, Claude Opus 5.5 at `medium` matches or exceeds Claude Opus 5 at `high` on coding and knowledge-work evaluations, and on several coding evaluations `low` comes close to it at much lower cost.", "why": "why a level carried over from another model is wrong"}
{"id": "e110", "source": "w4", "locator": "§Calibrate effort ¶2", "quote": "At a given level, Claude Opus 5.5 tends to think more per turn than Claude Opus 5, especially at `xhigh` and `max`. If you keep the `effort` value you set for Claude Opus 5, expect longer turns and more output tokens.", "why": "carried-over level costs more"}
{"id": "e111", "source": "w4", "locator": "§Calibrate effort ¶2 bullet 2", "quote": "Reserve `xhigh` and `max` for work where you've measured a quality gain.", "why": "step up only on measured gain"}
{"id": "e112", "source": "w4", "locator": "§Calibrate effort ¶2 bullet 3", "quote": "To get less thinking, lower the effort level first. Lowering effort reduces thinking, and with it cost and latency, more reliably than prompt instructions do.", "why": "effort before prompt wording when trimming"}
{"id": "e113", "source": "w4", "locator": "§Calibrate effort ¶2 bullet 1", "quote": "Thinking counts toward `max_tokens` even when thinking content isn't returned to you, so a limit sized for Claude Opus 5 with thinking off can cut replies off.", "why": "max_tokens room (API)"}
{"id": "e114", "source": "w4", "locator": "§Calibrate effort ¶3", "quote": "Changing the top-level `effort` value between requests invalidates the prompt cache.", "why": "cache cost of switching levels during a sweep (API)"}
{"id": "e115", "source": "w4", "locator": "§Calibrate effort ¶1", "quote": "[Effort](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/effort) is the main control for how much Claude Opus 5.5 thinks, and because thinking is always on, it's the first setting to adjust when trading off intelligence, latency, and cost.", "why": "effort is the first dial"}
{"id": "e116", "source": "w4", "locator": "§intro", "quote": "Claude Opus 5.5 generates output tokens more than 30 percent faster than Claude Opus 5 and tends to complete the same task with fewer tokens.", "why": "context for Opus 5.5 vs Opus 5"}
{"id": "e117", "source": "w5", "locator": "§Key takeaways", "quote": "Start with default effort levels for each model and tune as a general preference based on the type of work you do rather than task-by-task.", "why": "Claude Code blog: default first, tune per type of work"}
{"id": "e118", "source": "w5", "locator": "§Key takeaways", "quote": "If Claude has all the pertinent context, clearly tried, and still got it wrong, that's a signal to pick a more capable model. If Claude got it wrong by skipping a file, not running the tests, or bailing on a refactor partway through, pick a higher effort level.", "why": "the two-signal test for reading your own failures"}
{"id": "e119", "source": "w5", "locator": "§Claude Code effort level and model selection", "quote": "At a higher effort, Claude will take more of those actions (for example, read files, run tests, and double-check) before it comes back to you. At lower effort, it would rather ask you for more context than spend tokens figuring something out on its own.", "why": "same mechanism as w1, from a second author"}
{"id": "e120", "source": "w5", "locator": "§Picking an effort level", "quote": "Our guidance is that for most tasks you should use the model’s default effort level. The default is the level where Claude will scale its token usage according to what most people would want to spend on a task.", "why": "default is the baseline"}
{"id": "e121", "source": "w5", "locator": "§Picking an effort level", "quote": "Choose it deliberately when you have a strong preference for thoroughness or speed based on your domain or the type of work you do. Consider this more as a general preference than a task-by-task decision.", "why": "preference, not per task"}
{"id": "e122", "source": "w5", "locator": "§What to change when Claude gets it wrong", "quote": "When Claude gets something wrong, your first instinct shouldn’t be to adjust a knob, but to examine the context you have provided. Is your prompt too vague? Is Claude connected to the right tools? Equipped with the right skills?", "why": "check context before effort"}
{"id": "e123", "source": "w5", "locator": "§What to change when Claude gets it wrong", "quote": "If you're increasing effort on a task that shouldn't need it, the fix is often upstream, in your context, your CLAUDE.md, or how the task is scoped.", "why": "upstream fix"}
{"id": "e124", "source": "w5", "locator": "§What to change when Claude gets it wrong", "quote": "But assuming you have provided clear context and Claude still gets something wrong, the question to ask yourself is: did it not try hard enough, or did it not know enough?", "why": "the two questions"}
{"id": "e125", "source": "w5", "locator": "§Effort: Claude didn’t try hard enough", "quote": "Pick a higher effort level if Claude got it wrong by skipping a file, not running the tests, or not double-checking its work. This is most relevant if you selected an effort level below the model’s default.", "why": "raise effort signal"}
{"id": "e126", "source": "w5", "locator": "§Model: The problem was too hard", "quote": "A larger model is helpful for situations where the smaller model is confidently wrong no matter how much context you give it.", "why": "model, not effort, signal"}
{"id": "e127", "source": "w5", "locator": "§How Claude Code effort level works", "quote": "Claude will be more predisposed to double-checking additional hypotheses or verifying correctness at higher effort levels, but it generally won’t artificially inflate usage for simple tasks at higher effort levels.", "why": "high effort on simple task does not balloon"}
{"id": "e128", "source": "w5", "locator": "§Effort, model, and token consumption", "quote": "Another nuance to this: effort shapes token consumption but doesn't limit it. The only hard cap in the system is max_tokens, which truncates a response mid-stream when hit.", "why": "effort is not a budget"}
{"id": "e129", "source": "w5", "locator": "§Start with the defaults", "quote": "Most of the time, you shouldn't be thinking about either setting. When a result misses the mark, ask, “did Claude not know enough or did it not try hard enough?” and adjust as needed.", "why": "closing rule"}
{"id": "e130", "source": "w5", "locator": "§byline", "quote": "This article was written by Lydia Hallie, member of technical staff on the Claude Code team.", "why": "author standing"}
{"id": "e131", "source": "w5", "locator": "§Picking an effort level", "quote": "Some practical insight that may help guide you following the launch of Claude Opus 4.8: in our testing we found when you use the default effort setting for Opus 4.8, it will produce better results for about the same number of tokens when compared to using the default effort setting of Opus 4.7 for the same task.", "why": "dates the post: written for Opus 4.8, before Opus 5.5"}
{"id": "e132", "source": "w5", "locator": "§How Claude Code effort level works, figure caption", "quote": "Same prompt, two effort levels. The high effort path generates roughly 7x more tokens to reach a higher confidence answer.", "why": "illustrative 7x"}
{"id": "e133", "source": "w3", "locator": "§Recommended effort levels for Claude Opus 5.5", "quote": "Run an effort sweep on your own evals rather than carrying settings over from an earlier model, and set a large `max_tokens` at the higher levels: it's a hard limit on total output (thinking plus response text).", "why": "API doc repeats the sweep advice"}
{"id": "e135", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Choose an effort level", "quote": "To test levels against your own work, see [Calibrate effort](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/prompting-claude-opus-5-5#calibrate-effort) in the Opus 5.5 prompting guide.", "why": "Claude Code docs point to the guide for calibrating"}
{"id": "e136", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Adjust effort level", "quote": "1. An explicit choice: the [`CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL`](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/env-vars#variables) environment variable, launching with `--effort`, or `/effort` in the session", "why": "resolution order step 1"}
{"id": "e137", "source": "w2", "locator": "§Adjust effort level", "quote": "2. Your settings: the level you saved for the model or an [`effortLevel`](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/settings-reference#effortlevel) key", "why": "resolution order step 2"}
{"id": "e138", "source": "w1", "locator": "§intro", "quote": "but I’ve received a lot of questions on this from users. What is effort really, and when do you use which effort level?", "why": "why the author wrote the post"}
{"id": "e139", "source": "w1", "locator": "§How effort levels impact output", "quote": "In the run I traced, it adversarially reviewed its first draft, then read the installed parser’s source to check for bugs, ran many clean test cases until they gave the same output as the input, ran a standard XSS test suite, and finally, wrote a random-document fuzzer.", "why": "html-js-filter high run, full sequence"}
{"id": "e134", "source": "w5", "locator": "§What to change, figure caption", "quote": "Two questions, one fallback. Use the heuristic to pick a starting point, not a hard rule.", "why": "the heuristic is a starting point"}
{"id": "e140", "source": "w1", "locator": "§When to use different effort levels", "quote": "Try varying effort for Opus 5.5 and Fable 5.1 based on your task or even mid-conversation by using /effort in Claude Code and let me know if this matches your intuition.", "why": "author invites per-task switching; contrasts with w5's preference framing"}
{"id": "e141", "source": "w1", "locator": "§How effort levels impact output", "quote": "I found I was surprised by the scope and ambition of a lot of these tasks. They are much more complicated than the average task I’d face.", "why": "benchmark tasks are harder than daily work"}
{"id": "e142", "source": "w1", "locator": "§gsea-proteomics", "quote": "At low effort, Claude picked a reasonable-sounding way to prep the data, ran the analysis that one way, and reported the result.", "why": "gsea low"}
